-- Q960: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 172(a) and (b) as amended by the TCJA, NOLs arising after December 31, 2017 may be carried forward indefinitely but are limited to offsetting 80% of taxable income in any carryforward year. The TCJA also eliminated the two-year carryback for most taxpayers, with limited exceptions for farming losses under Section 172(b)(1)(B).
Wrong (B): The two-year carryback with 20-year carryforward and no income limitation was the pre-TCJA rule, which no longer applies to post-2017 NOLs.
Wrong (C): Post-TCJA NOLs do not expire after five years; they carry forward indefinitely but are subject to the 80% limitation rather than being unlimited.
Wrong (D): NOLs are not eliminated. They remain available as indefinite carryforwards, just with the 80% income offset limitation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 960;

-- Q961: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 461(l), noncorporate taxpayers may not deduct aggregate net business losses exceeding the threshold amount ($305,000 for single filers in 2026, indexed annually). Dr. Whitfield''s $750,000 loss minus the $305,000 threshold leaves $445,000 of excess business loss. This excess is recharacterized as an NOL carryforward under Section 172, subject to the 80% taxable income limitation in subsequent years.
Wrong (A): Excess business losses are not permanently disallowed. They are recharacterized as NOL carryforwards available in future years.
Wrong (B): Section 461(l) applies regardless of whether the taxpayer materially participates in the business activity. Active trade or business status does not exempt a taxpayer from the excess business loss limitation.
Wrong (C): The excess is treated as an NOL carryforward, not limited to offset against capital gains. It follows standard NOL carryforward rules under Section 172.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 961;

-- Q962: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.1502-13(c), gains on intercompany transactions within a consolidated group are deferred and taken into account under the matching rule. For depreciable property, the $40,000 deferred gain ($120,000 minus $80,000 basis) is recognized over the asset''s remaining useful life as the buying member claims depreciation on the higher purchase price.
Wrong (A): Gains on intercompany sales are not recognized immediately. The matching rule defers recognition until a corresponding event occurs, such as depreciation or disposition outside the group.
Wrong (C): Intercompany gains are not permanently excluded. They are deferred and eventually recognized through the matching or acceleration rules.
Wrong (D): The treatment depends on the consolidated return regulations, not on whether the parent files separately. Gains are deferred within the group regardless of filing status.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 962;

